          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When a reference note names a root like K21, what do I look for to get to the specific code?",
          "answer": "Your reference note gives you a three-character category, K21. The corpus I searched explains the lookup method but does not list the K21 subcodes, so I can't name the specific code from it.\n\n**The two-step method.** Find the term in the Alphabetic Index, then confirm the code in the Tabular List. The index does not carry the instructional notes, so skipping the Tabular List is a common cause of wrong codes. The Tabular List governs. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\n**Check the character count.** Most categories are not valid at three characters. Carry the code to its full length. If a category needs a seventh character but the earlier positions are empty, use the placeholder X to hold those positions open. (Same source.)\n\n**Read the notes.** Excludes1 means the two conditions cannot both be present and the codes are never reported together. Other notes in the Tabular List may also apply. (Same source.)\n\n**Laterality and episode modifiers.** Many categories distinguish right, left, bilateral and unspecified. Injury codes often carry a seventh character for the episode of care: initial encounter, subsequent encounter or sequela. These features are common in the musculoskeletal and injury chapters. K21 is not described as using them in the passages I retrieved. (Source: *ICD-10-CM and WHO ICD-10*.)\n\n**Accompanying codes.** For reflux disease, the retrieved passages say that long-term drug therapy status, body mass index, tobacco and alcohol use are coded from other chapters. They also say dysphagia is not routinely part of uncomplicated reflux disease and is reported in addition when documented. (Source: *Coding gastroesophageal reflux disease: K21 and the esophagitis distinction*.)\n\n**What I could not confirm.** The passages did not show which K21 subcodes exist, what separates them (for example, with or without esophagitis), or how to pick between them. Check the current Tabular List at the K21 entry, and the Alphabetic Index under \"reflux\" and \"esophagitis.\" These code sets are revised each year, effective October 1, so confirm against the version that applies to your date of service. If this is for billing or records, a trained medical coder should make the final selection.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
